---
import DocsLayout from '../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import SpecTable from '../../components/SpecTable.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import { url } from '../../utils/paths';

const pageTitle = 'Operations & troubleshooting';
const pageDesc =
  'Runbook for AutoTelegramForward: read the health board, query attributable log history, handle FloodWait, resolve database contention, replay dead-lettered jobs and roll back safely.';

const toc = [
  { id: 'health', title: 'Health checks' },
  { id: 'logs', title: 'Log history' },
  { id: 'floodwait', title: 'FloodWait' },
  { id: 'database', title: 'Database contention' },
  { id: 'dlq', title: 'Dead-letter queue' },
  { id: 'backup', title: 'Backup & rollback' },
  { id: 'upgrade', title: 'Upgrades' },
];

const logSignals = [
  ['[PIPELINE:ROUTE]', 'A task matched this source message.', 'Normal. Check the task list if you expected a different target.'],
  ['[PIPELINE:FILTER_DROP]', 'A filter rejected the message.', 'Expected only if the rule says so — inspect the task policy.'],
  ['[PIPELINE:CLEAN]', 'Cleaner removed content.', 'Normal when cleaning is enabled.'],
  ['[PIPELINE:REWRITE]', 'The AI pass rewrote the text.', 'If it did not run, check the task toggle and provider reachability.'],
  ['[PIPELINE:RESTRICTED_BYPASS]', 'Protected content was re-uploaded instead of forwarded.', 'Normal copy-protection handling.'],
  ['[PIPELINE:QUEUE]', 'The job was persisted with a lease.', 'A job stuck here means workers are not draining.'],
  ['[PIPELINE:DELIVERED]', 'Telegram accepted the delivery.', 'Done.'],
  ['[PIPELINE:RETRY_WAIT]', 'The job is waiting out its backoff.', 'A paused task keeps jobs here by design.'],
  ['[PIPELINE:DLQ]', 'Retries were exhausted.', 'Read the recorded error, fix the cause, then replay.'],
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title={pageTitle}
  description={pageDesc}
  path="/operations/"
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: 'Home', href: url('/') },
    { name: 'Reference', href: url('/api/') },
    { name: 'Operations', href: url('/operations/') },
  ]}
  toc={toc}
  keywords={['AutoTelegramForward troubleshooting', 'Telegram FloodWait handling', 'dead letter queue replay']}
>
  <p>
    This is the page you open when something is wrong. Every section ends in a command or an endpoint you can actually
    run, and the log signals below are the vocabulary the stack logs in.
  </p>

  <section class="not-prose scroll-mt-24" id="health">
    <h2 class="display-3 text-white mb-3 scroll-mt-24">Health checks</h2>
    <p class="m-0 mb-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
      Start broad, then narrow. The CLI status board answers "is anything red", the readiness endpoint answers "is this
      deployment configured", and doctor answers "why".
    </p>
    <div class="grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <CodeBlock
        label="Three levels of diagnosis"
        language="bash"
        content={`atf status             # live board: ports, reachability, task counts
atf validate --strict  # config, compose, catalog parity, secrets scan
atf doctor --fix       # deep checks across DB, binaries, ports, gRPC

# The same, over HTTP
curl -s localhost:8080/api/health/services | jq
curl -s localhost:8080/api/system/setup | jq '.ready, .env'`}
      />
      <SpecTable
        headers={['Signal', 'Means']}
        rows={[
          ['<code>ready: false</code>', 'At least one required environment key is missing or a service is unreachable.'],
          ['<code>core: false</code>', 'The Python core is down — nothing is being read from Telegram.'],
          ['<code>logger: false</code>', 'Log endpoints answer 502; delivery continues but history is not being recorded.'],
          ['<code>api: false</code>', 'Clients cannot reach the control plane.'],
          ['<code>web: false</code>', 'Only the dashboard is affected; the bot and API still work.'],
        ]}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="not-prose scroll-mt-24" id="logs">
    <h2 class="display-3 text-white mb-3 scroll-mt-24">Log history</h2>
    <p class="m-0 mb-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
      History is attributable: entries name the actor, the service, the level, the subject task and the correlation id.
      That is what lets you answer "who changed this, and what happened next" hours later.
    </p>
    <div class="grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2 mb-4">
      <CodeBlock
        label="Query history from the CLI"
        language="bash"
        content={`atf logs tail --follow -n 50     # live stream
atf logs query --level ERROR     # filtered query
atf logs stats                   # counts by service and level
atf logs export --format csv --out logs.csv`}
      />
      <CodeBlock
        label="Query history over HTTP"
        language="bash"
        content={`# Bounded paging, tenant-scoped server-side
curl -s "localhost:8080/api/logs?service=core&level=ERROR&limit=50" \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" | jq '.items'

# Aggregates, one task's history, and export
curl -s localhost:8080/api/logs/stats -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" | jq
curl -s localhost:8080/api/rules/<id>/logs -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" | jq
curl -s localhost:8080/api/logs/export -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" -o logs.json`}
      />
    </div>

    <SpecTable headers={['Signal', 'Meaning', 'What to do']} rows={logSignals} caption="Pipeline log vocabulary" />
  </section>

  <section class="not-prose scroll-mt-24" id="floodwait">
    <h2 class="display-3 text-white mb-3 scroll-mt-24">FloodWait</h2>
    <p class="m-0 mb-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
      Telegram rate-limits accounts, and fighting it is how accounts get banned. ATF treats a FloodWait as a scheduling
      signal: the account is marked time-limited, its deliveries pause, jobs stay queued, and everything resumes when
      the window closes.
    </p>
    <ol class="grid gap-2 list-none p-0 m-0">
      <li class="flex gap-2.5 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="check" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-emerald-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.4} />
        <span>The client pool records the affected account and the moment it becomes usable again.</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2.5 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="check" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-emerald-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.4} />
        <span>Workers skip that account and keep serving the others — one limited account does not stall the installation.</span>
      </li>
      <li class="flex gap-2.5 text-[14.5px] text-slate-300">
        <Icon name="check" class="w-4 h-4 mt-[3px] text-emerald-400 shrink-0" strokeWidth={2.4} />
        <span>When the window expires the queue drains automatically. Repeated FloodWaits mean your rates are too high, not that the code is broken.</span>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="not-prose scroll-mt-24" id="database">
    <h2 class="display-3 text-white mb-3 scroll-mt-24">Database contention</h2>
    <p class="m-0 mb-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
      Under a burst of concurrent tasks, SQLite can report <code>database is locked</code> if writes pile up. The fix is
      either a better journal mode or a real database engine.
    </p>
    <div class="grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <CodeBlock
        label="Keep SQLite, make it concurrent"
        language="bash"
        content={`sqlite3 data/atf.db "PRAGMA journal_mode=WAL; PRAGMA busy_timeout=5000;"
atf db status          # integrity and lock visibility
atf db snapshot        # consistent copy before changing anything`}
      />
      <CodeBlock
        label="Move to PostgreSQL"
        language="bash"
        content={`# .env
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://atf_user:password@localhost:5432/atf_db

atf db snapshot
atf system services    # restart the stack
atf doctor             # confirm every service reconnected`}
      />
    </div>
    <Callout type="warn" class="mt-4" title="Do not delete the WAL files to unlock a database">
      <p>
        Removing <code>-wal</code>/<code>-shm</code> files while the stack is running can lose writes that were never
        checkpointed. Stop the stack first, or raise <code>busy_timeout</code> and let it settle.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section class="not-prose scroll-mt-24" id="dlq">
    <h2 class="display-3 text-white mb-3 scroll-mt-24">Dead-letter queue</h2>
    <p class="m-0 mb-4 text-[15px] leading-relaxed text-slate-300">
      A job that exhausts its retries stops guessing and lands in the DLQ with the error that killed it. Replay is
      deliberate: fix the cause, then replay, one job or all of them.
    </p>
    <div class="grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <CodeBlock
        label="Inspect and replay"
        language="bash"
        content={`atf data queue            # depth and in-flight jobs
atf data dlq              # list dead-lettered jobs with errors
atf data dlq --replay     # replay once the cause is fixed`}
      />
      <CodeBlock
        label="The same over HTTP"
        language="bash"
        content={`curl -s localhost:8080/api/queue -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" | jq
curl -s localhost:8080/api/dlq -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" | jq '.items'
curl -s -X POST localhost:8080/api/dlq/<id>/retry \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" | jq`}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="not-prose scroll-mt-24" id="backup">
    <h2 class="display-3 text-white mb-3 scroll-mt-24">Backup & rollback</h2>
    <div class="grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <CodeBlock
        label="Encrypted export / import"
        language="bash"
        content={`atf data backup-export --out atf-$(date +%F).atf
atf db snapshot --out data-snapshot.db
atf sessions backup --out sessions.bak

# Restore on this host or another
atf data backup-import --in atf-2026-10-16.atf`}
      />
      <CodeBlock
        label="Roll back an upgrade"
        language="bash"
        content={`# Pin the previous tag, rebuild what changed
git fetch --tags
git checkout v1.5.2
docker compose build && docker compose up -d

atf validate --strict
curl -f localhost:8080/healthz`}
      />
    </div>
    <Callout type="tip" class="mt-4" title="Config without secrets">
      <p>
        When you only need the shape of an installation — for review or on a ticket — export configuration without
        secrets and keep credentials where they belong.
      </p>
    </Callout>
  </section>

  <section class="not-prose scroll-mt-24" id="upgrade">
    <h2 class="display-3 text-white mb-3 scroll-mt-24">Upgrades</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      label="The gated path"
      language="bash"
      content={`atf validate --strict    # refuse to start from a broken state
atf system upgrade       # pull, rebuild, restart, verify
atf status --watch       # keep an eye on the board afterwards
atf release verify       # manifest + portable build integrity`}
    />
    <p class="mt-4 text-[13px] text-slate-400">
      Release-by-release notes live on the <a class="text-sky-400" href={url('/releases/')}>releases page</a>; the
      command surface is documented in the <a class="text-sky-400" href={url('/cli/')}>CLI reference</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</DocsLayout>
